Logging Cheat Sheet: Attacks on Logs (OWASP Cheat Sheet Series)
is available on the OWASP Log Injection page.

To prevent an attacker from writing malicious content into the application log, apply defenses such as: - Do not use unstructured text log formats. In many logging frameworks, unstructured text log formats are susceptible to log entry injection through Carriage Return (CR) or Line Feed (LF) characters (see CWE-93). Some logging frameworks do not consider log injections through CRLF in unstructured logs to be a vulnerability (which means that this will not be fixed).
- Instead, use structured log formats, such as JSON.
- Limit the size of the user input value used to create the log message.

Log Injection in Java

In Java, do not use string concatenation to build log messages:

~~~java
// BAD: placeholders in `username` can change which values fill the template.
logger.warn("Failure for user " + username + " and role {}.", role, ex);

// OK
logger.warn("Failure for user {} and role {}.", username, role, ex);
~~~

Network segmentation Cheat Sheet: Introduction (OWASP Cheat Sheet Series)
Network segmentation is the core of multi-layer defense in depth for modern services. Segmentation slow down an attacker if he cannot implement attacks such as:

- SQL-injections, see [SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.md);
- compromise of workstations of employees with elevated privileges;
- compromise of another server in the perimeter of the organization;
- compromise of the target service through the compromise of the LDAP directory, DNS server, and other corporate services and sites published on the Internet.

The main goal of this cheat sheet is to show the basics of network segmentation to effectively counter attacks by building a secure and maximally isolated service network architecture.

